3},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294b43d7-413d-5eb9-8165-9cef5bfe55d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333371} {:answer/id #uuid "2e21978e-9fea-539c-9508-dbcb1f252e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333418} {:answer/id #uuid "7929794f-8d5a-5b80-b14b-ded52e774e58" :answer/type