lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ada6e26-3e25-5024-8918-75ece9936f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109916} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4c31b7-948e-5ad7-b087-0fe89630298e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109917} {:answer/id #uuid "4c34414e-2009-5cf3-b56c-4690e524521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109918} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4d65ad-f84e-57b5-af67-61b03c8dcd51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 